diff --git a/docs/dflash-conditioning.md b/docs/dflash-conditioning.md
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/dflash-conditioning.md
@@ -0,0 +1,32 @@
+# DFlash2 recent context with KVMem
+
+When KVMem is enabled with DFlash2, Engine startup resolves the effective recent span
+to at least the model's draft sliding-window capacity, rounded up to 64-token pages.
+For a 2048-token ring, requested recent64 or2000 becomes2048. A larger existing
+recent setting keeps its normal downward page rounding. Other speculative backends
+and runs without KVMem retain their requested setting.
+
+This uses the existing recent policy for both Query-required pages and resident-only
+Pressure. It adds no second page-pinning policy or cold recall to tool continuation.
+`KVMEM_DFLASH_RECENT requested=... effective=... ring=...` reports any increase, and
+the resolved Engine options carry the effective value.
+
+The selected budget B is unchanged. Keeping more recent pages leaves less room for
+other retrieved history. B must hold sink plus the effective recent span; with the
+default one-page sink and a2048-token ring, B must be at least2112. An executing
+Engine rejects a smaller budget explicitly. Budget planning uses the same resolved
+span as its minimum even if its seed B is smaller.
+
+The restored draft floor is bounded by its committed frontier, including immediately
+before DFlash2 decode. A truncating restore can therefore hide unavailable history
+without leaving a future lower bound. This guard does not expand the valid interval.
+
+Draft KV remains BF16. There is no INT8 draft storage, shadow buffer, new request
+protocol or ingestion state in this change. Recent=2048 already covers a2048-token
+ring: the minimum is idempotent, not an additional2048-token allocation.
+
+The ring-retention and restored-floor ideas originate in
+melis2023/ninfer-kvmem-actkv commits
+`f9cfb15dfa7251707d8f114c29ecb0f52eea62e6` and
+`e7996f7c3894bcfcef4b1a3c2d3ad832646f20d5`. This integration normalizes recent once
+instead of maintaining independent floor and page-pinning overrides.
